                            HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   2024–: Aperture Civic.

“Same address,” said Sofía, “on every registered-agent filing. The name
changes every forty years like a snake changing skin.” She tapped the
left edge of the paper. “And look at the signatures.”
   In the right-hand margin she’d taped photographs, scans, faces,
small and gray and square. The earliest was a man with a high collar
and a ledger. Josiah Vale, Treasurer, 1924. The next was a thin man in a
brown suit with a notebook in his breast pocket. Dr. Anthony Vale,
Founder, Meridian Acoustics, 1968. Then a man in short sleeves and a tie.
Alan Vale, Community Outreach, 1991. Then a tall, gray, courteous man
in a good coat, holding a pair of oversized scissors.
   I stood in front of the thin man in the brown suit for a long time.
   I’d seen him. I’d seen the exact way he held his head. In the lobby of
a bank in 1962, at the next desk over from a couple who’d just been told
no. Writing the address down. And at the dark end of a record-shop
counter in 1973, ticking off a boy with two copies of the same record.
   And the short sleeves and the tie. I’d seen him once, too, on the edge
of a path under the oaks, counting kids.
   “It’s a family,” I said.
   “It’s a family,” said Sofía. “The Vales. For a hundred years and more.
The founders are at the top of the wall and the cousins are at the bot‐
tom, and every one of them keeps a notebook. I found it in a society
column. It’s a family tradition, they teach it to the boys, they call it
‘keeping the count.’” She said it carefully, like a woman handling a live
wire. “I want to be careful. Nobody has to call a meeting. A family
business can keep the same hobby for a hundred years. The banks
drew the line. The city drew the line. The Vales bought on the right
side of it and wrote down who stayed.”
   Manny, who had been eating an empanada, said with his mouth
full, “That’s the worst hobby I ever heard.”
   “Why does a company that sells office white noise survey New‐
grange?” I said.




